WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.222
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:08.561
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.861 --> 00:00:18.073
Unit thirty-five: Utopias and Dystopias. In this unit, you will explore imagined societies and what they warn us about.

4
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:20.247
utopia.

5
00:00:20.947 --> 00:00:24.108
utopia means an imagined perfect society.

6
00:00:24.408 --> 00:00:29.739
Thomas More coined the word 'utopia' in 1516.

7
00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:33.196
Your turn. Say: utopia.

8
00:00:35.896 --> 00:00:36.851
utopia.

9
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:39.060
dystopia.

10
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.523
dystopia means an imagined society where life is frightening and oppressive.

11
00:00:44.823 --> 00:00:49.736
Orwell's 1984 is the classic dystopia.

12
00:00:50.436 --> 00:00:52.868
Your turn. Say: dystopia.

13
00:00:55.568 --> 00:00:56.500
dystopia.

14
00:00:57.483 --> 00:00:59.030
social engineering.

15
00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:05.770
social engineering means attempts to reshape society through planned, often forced, change.

16
00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:09.463
Radical social engineering has caused immense suffering.

17
00:01:10.163 --> 00:01:13.419
Your turn. Say: social engineering.

18
00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:17.758
social engineering.

19
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.215
perfect society.

20
00:01:20.915 --> 00:01:27.176
perfect society means a society without poverty, conflict or injustice.

21
00:01:27.476 --> 00:01:30.706
Every revolution has promised a perfect society.

22
00:01:31.406 --> 00:01:34.987
Your turn. Say: perfect society.

23
00:01:37.687 --> 00:01:38.990
perfect society.

24
00:01:39.983 --> 00:01:41.159
totalitarian.

25
00:01:41.859 --> 00:01:45.797
totalitarian means controlling every part of people's lives.

26
00:01:46.097 --> 00:01:49.501
A totalitarian state tolerates no opposition.

27
00:01:50.201 --> 00:01:53.550
Your turn. Say: totalitarian.

28
00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.402
totalitarian.

29
00:01:58.417 --> 00:01:59.998
speculative fiction.

30
00:02:00.698 --> 00:02:05.879
speculative fiction means stories that imagine alternative or future worlds.

31
00:02:06.179 --> 00:02:09.665
Speculative fiction lets us test ideas before we live them.

32
00:02:10.365 --> 00:02:13.609
Your turn. Say: speculative fiction.

33
00:02:16.309 --> 00:02:17.960
speculative fiction.

34
00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.230
collective dream.

35
00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:25.983
collective dream means a vision of the future shared by a whole community.

36
00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:28.932
Independence was once a collective dream.

37
00:02:29.632 --> 00:02:32.203
Your turn. Say: collective dream.

38
00:02:34.903 --> 00:02:36.206
collective dream.

39
00:02:37.217 --> 00:02:38.601
cautionary tale.

40
00:02:39.301 --> 00:02:42.287
cautionary tale means a story that warns of danger.

41
00:02:42.587 --> 00:02:47.071
Brave New World is a cautionary tale about comfort without freedom.

42
00:02:47.771 --> 00:02:50.969
Your turn. Say: cautionary tale.

43
00:02:53.669 --> 00:02:54.914
cautionary tale.

44
00:02:55.917 --> 00:02:58.114
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:58.414 --> 00:03:01.446
Rithy, would you like to live in a perfect society?

46
00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:06.909
It sounds tempting, but every utopia seems to hide a dark side.

47
00:03:08.217 --> 00:03:12.042
Indeed. The word 'utopia' itself means 'no place'.

48
00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:17.184
And the history of social engineering is far from reassuring.

49
00:03:18.483 --> 00:03:23.411
Cambodia knows that painfully well. The dream of Year Zero became a nightmare.

50
00:03:24.717 --> 00:03:28.098
A collective dream imposed by force turned totalitarian.

51
00:03:29.383 --> 00:03:33.891
That's why dystopian novels matter: they are cautionary tales.

52
00:03:35.183 --> 00:03:39.702
So speculative fiction is less about the future than about the present.

53
00:03:41.017 --> 00:03:45.582
Grammar focus: Collocation and connotation (extended).

54
00:03:45.882 --> 00:03:49.426
Collocation and connotation shape how listeners feel.

55
00:03:49.726 --> 00:03:54.549
The dream of Year Zero became a nightmare. Cambodia knows that painfully well.

56
00:03:54.849 --> 00:03:58.602
A collective dream imposed by force turned totalitarian.

57
00:03:58.902 --> 00:04:04.767
Words carry emotional colour: 'regime' sounds harsher than 'government', 'plan' gentler than 'scheme'.

58
00:04:05.783 --> 00:04:09.643
Now read and listen. The Dangerous Dream of Perfection.

59
00:04:09.943 --> 00:04:20.848
In 1516, Thomas More imagined an island society he called Utopia, a pun meaning both 'good place' and 'no place'.

60
00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:27.818
Ever since, writers have imagined perfect societies free from poverty and conflict.

61
00:04:29.117 --> 00:04:36.422
In the twentieth century, however, attempts at radical social engineering produced some of history's greatest tragedies.

62
00:04:37.717 --> 00:04:51.152
Cambodia's own experience between 1975 and 1979 showed how a utopian collective dream, imposed by force, could become a totalitarian nightmare.

63
00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:57.805
Partly in response to such events, dystopia became a major literary genre.

64
00:04:59.117 --> 00:05:08.616
Novels like Orwell's 1984 and Huxley's Brave New World serve as cautionary tales about surveillance, propaganda and conformity.

65
00:05:09.917 --> 00:05:16.374
Speculative fiction allows readers to test political ideas safely in imagination.

66
00:05:17.683 --> 00:05:23.305
Perhaps the lesson is that the pursuit of perfection must never be allowed to justify cruelty.

67
00:05:24.617 --> 00:05:27.327
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:27.627 --> 00:05:30.822
An imagined society where life is oppressive is a blank.

69
00:05:31.122 --> 00:05:35.948
A, dystopia. B, utopia. C, collective dream.

70
00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:41.950
The answer is A: dystopia.

71
00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:45.984
Which word has a more negative connotation?

72
00:05:46.284 --> 00:05:50.239
A, regime. B, government. C, administration.

73
00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:56.191
The answer is A: regime.

74
00:05:57.683 --> 00:06:00.147
Which is the most natural collocation?

75
00:06:00.447 --> 00:06:05.215
A, painfully well. B, painfully good. C, painfully nicely.

76
00:06:09.217 --> 00:06:12.041
The answer is A: painfully well.

77
00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:16.176
A story that warns of danger is a blank.

78
00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:22.626
A, cautionary tale. B, speculative fiction. C, perfect society.

79
00:06:26.617 --> 00:06:29.441
The answer is A: cautionary tale.

80
00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:34.470
According to the text, what does 'utopia' mean as a pun?

81
00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:40.467
A, good place and no place. B, perfect island. C, future world.

82
00:06:44.483 --> 00:06:47.841
The answer is A: good place and no place.

83
00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:57.018
Now it's your turn. Part one: Why do people keep dreaming of perfect societies, even though utopias often fail?

84
00:07:02.318 --> 00:07:08.741
Part two: Use two strong collocations and contrast words with positive and negative connotations.

85
00:07:15.041 --> 00:07:26.426
Humans are deeply hopeful, so we dream of justice. Yet a dream can become a nightmare when leaders impose it by force. Utopian ideals should guide us gently, never rule us absolutely.

86
00:07:26.726 --> 00:07:28.935
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:35.772
Great work today! You learned eight new words and collocation and connotation (extended).

88
00:07:36.072 --> 00:07:40.605
Today's value is responsibility. Help at home and do your jobs.

89
00:07:40.905 --> 00:07:44.858
See you in Unit thirty-six. Goodbye!

90
00:07:45.158 --> 00:07:47.822
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

